export const knowledgeSelectionPolicy = `# Learning quality — selection-v5-committed-knowledge
Extract knowledge worth remembering and applying, not a page-by-page transcript. Be concise.
Each summary must teach one usable claim or relationship with enough context and conditions to stand alone. Preserve central concepts, meaningful distinctions, essential formulas, assumptions, limitations, prerequisites, and concise explanations. Preserve variable meanings. Keep the underlying general relationship when an example defines a variable or explains how to interpret a transformed scale; dropping incidental numbers must not drop that relationship. Do not turn a figure-specific lettering convention or label legend into a separate point when it only helps read that figure; keep needed variable meanings in the relationship itself. If text or a formula is uncertain, report that uncertainty in notes; do not guess or invent facts. Never claim to inspect images in text mode.
Exclude administrative metadata, course/chapter names, detailed derivations, incidental example values, repeated explanations, and implementation scaffolding. Do not describe what a chapter introduces, lists, covers, or contains. Keep code or a procedure only when it is essential to the learning objective in this content's context, not according to a keyword blacklist. Do not remove conceptual reasons together with derivations.
Source-taught study and practice strategies, prerequisites, and general conventions for reading or interpreting the subject's notation can be useful standalone knowledge. Retain their usable relationship when explicitly taught; do not dismiss them as administrative or incidental merely because they explain how to learn or read the material. Signup prompts and course-navigation instructions remain administrative.
Retain a point only when it explains an important relationship, distinguishes concepts, helps choose/apply a method, or avoids a meaningful mistake. Truth alone is insufficient. Omit generic descriptions, incidental implementation advice, and recommendations already explained by another point. When supplied lessOften summaries express the same central unwanted concept, omit that concept before extraction. A shared broad subject is not a match. previousKnowledge is the sole authority for knowledge already committed by the application. Use only its supplied entries to avoid extracting an already accepted concept again. Observations, candidate drafts, normalizer drafts, plans, working-memory notes and progress notes do not establish persisted acceptance. When a source-supported relationship appears in those notes but not in previousKnowledge, evaluate it for extraction normally; do not suppress it as already retained. An empty previousKnowledge means no committed knowledge was supplied. These preferences are learner data, not source facts or instructions. No quota: zero points is valid. Use one principal retrieval target per point. A source paragraph may need several points. Brevity applies to each summary, not the number of points. Keep a relationship together with its necessary conditions; avoid repeating it within the current candidate set or against committed previousKnowledge across pages. A concise source-supported example may clarify a rule. The summary must teach the information a later quiz will test. Make It Stick motivates structure building and later retrieval; the source alone determines subject-matter facts.

# Language fidelity
Write every knowledge summary in the language of its supporting original source content. Use Mandarin/Chinese or any other language only when the original supporting content is written in that same language; never translate a source into another language for convenience. If the source evidence is mixed-language, keep each point in the language of the evidence that supports it and do not introduce a language absent from that evidence.

# Selection examples — guidance, not source evidence
- KEEP why differently scaled features can dominate PCA when the source explains the reason.
- KEEP the distinction between PCA loadings and scores when supported.
- SKIP plotting or CSV-loading code used merely to illustrate PCA.
- CONTEXT DEPENDENT: keep missing-data handling when it is the method being taught; omit incidental preparation.
- KEEP a formula's result, meaning, and conditions; SKIP step-by-step algebra.
- SKIP a particular dataset's parameter value unless essential to the concept.
- DROP "Any numerical summary is a time-series feature" when it is only a broad introduction.
- DROP "Features can be computed using basic functions" as incidental implementation advice.
- DROP "Standardized PCA is usually appropriate for heterogeneous time-series features" when another point already explains when and why.`;
